<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lindisfarne</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Holy Island</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lindisfarne, or Holy Island, is a tidal island off the coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northumberland">Northumberland</a> in north-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. In 635 the Irish monk Aidan founded a monastery there with the support of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oswald-of-northumbria">Oswald of Northumbria</a>, and it became a centre of Christian mission and learning, home of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-cuthbert">Saint Cuthbert</a> and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne-gospels">Lindisfarne Gospels</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raid on the monastery in 793 is often taken as the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/viking-age">Viking Age</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lindisfarne.jpg" alt="Lindisfarne" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chris Combe from York, UK · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Holy_Island_Sunrise_(again)_(15064111624).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
